rson</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidney Hill is the Tadodaho, the chief who presides over the Grand Council of the Haudenosaunee, chosen from the Onondaga Nation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidney-holland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidney-holland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidney Holland</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidney Holland was Prime Minister of New Zealand from 1949 to 1957, leading the First National Government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidney-shippard"><span class="enc-name">Sidney Shippard</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Sidney Shippard was a British colonial official and judge in southern Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sido"><span class="enc-name">Sido</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sido was a Suebian king of the 1st century CE who, with his brother Vangio, overthrew their uncle Vannius in 50 CE and shared his kingdom north of the Danube.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidoarjo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidoarjo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidoarjo</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidoarjo is a town and regency in East Java, Indonesia, on the Brantas delta just south of Surabaya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidon</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidon, or Saida, is a city on the Mediterranean coast of southern Lebanon, about 40 km south of Beirut, inhabited since prehistoric times.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidon-sea-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidon-sea-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidon Sea Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sidon Sea Castle is a crusader fortress built in 1228 on a small island off the harbour of Sidon in Lebanon, linked to the shore by a causeway.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidonio-pais"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidonio-pais.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidónio Pais</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidónio Pais was a Portuguese army officer and politician who seized power in a coup in December 1917 and ruled the First Portuguese Republic as an authoritarian…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidonius-apollinaris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sidonius-apollinaris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sidonius Apollinaris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidonius Apollinaris was a Gallo-Roman aristocrat, poet and bishop of Clermont from about 470.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidqa"><span class="enc-name">Sidqa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidqa was king of the Philistine city of Ashkelon in the late 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sidray-ibn-wazir"><span class="enc-name">Sidray ibn Wazir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sidray ibn Wazir was a commander of the Banu Wazir family in the west of al-Andalus who, in the revolt against the Almoravids in 1144, took Beja and Évora and ruled…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siebengebirge"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siebengebirge.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siebengebirge</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siebengebirge ('Seven Mountains') is a range of volcanic hills on the east bank of the Rhine south-east of Bonn, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sieg-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sieg-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sieg (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sieg is a river in western Germany, about 155 km long, rising in the Rothaar Mountains and flowing west through the Siegerland to join the Rhine near Bonn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-capability"><span class="enc-name">Siege capability</span><span class="enc-type">Historical factor</span><span class="enc-snip">Siege capability refers to the recurring pattern in which an empire's ability to capture fortified cities determined how far it could expand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-engine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege engine</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A siege engine is a device designed to break or bypass fortifications, such as a battering ram, siege tower, catapult or trebuchet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-acre-1189-1191"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Acre (1189–1191)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1189–1191</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Acre (1189–1191) was a long siege of the port city of Acre during the Third Crusade.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-akhulgo"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Akhulgo</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1839</span><span class="enc-snip">The siege of Akhulgo was a Russian siege of Imam Shamil's mountain fortress in Dagestan in the summer of 1839.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-alamut"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-alamut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Alamut</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1256</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Alamut was the capture of the mountain fortress of the Nizari Ismailis in northern Iran by Mongol forces under Hulagu in 1256.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-amida-359"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Amida (359)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 359 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Amida in 359 CE was the capture of the Roman city of Amida, modern Diyarbakir, by the Sasanian king Shapur II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-antioch-256"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Antioch (256)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 256 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Antioch in 256 CE was the capture of the great Roman city of Antioch by the Sasanian king Shapur I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-baghdad-1258.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1258</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Baghdad in 1258 was the capture and sack of the Abbasid capital by the Mongol army of Hulagu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-basra-1775-1776"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Basra (1775–1776)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1775–1776</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Basra (1775–1776) was the capture of the Ottoman-held port city of Basra by the forces of the Zand Empire under Karim Khan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-belgrade-1521"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Belgrade (1521)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1521</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Belgrade in 1521 was the capture of the Hungarian fortress city by the Ottoman sultan Suleiman the Magnificent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-bursa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-bursa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Bursa</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1326</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Bursa was the long Ottoman blockade of the Byzantine city of Bursa in north-western Anatolia, which surrendered in 1326 to Orhan, son of Osman I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-caizhou"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Caizhou</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1233–1234</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Caizhou in 1233 and 1234 was the final campaign against the Jurchen Jin dynasty in China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-calvi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-calvi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Calvi</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1794</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Calvi was fought from June to August 1794, when British forces and Corsican allies besieged the French garrison of Calvi in north-western Corsica.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-carthage-149-146-bce"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Carthage (149–146 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 146 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Carthage was the main engagement of the Third Punic War, in which Roman forces besieged the city of Carthage.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-djenne"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Djenné</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · c. 1473</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Djenné was a long siege of the trading city of Djenné in the inner Niger delta by the Songhai ruler Sunni Ali.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-dorostolon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-dorostolon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Dorostolon</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 971</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Dorostolon took place from April to July 971 at Dorostolon, now Silistra, on the lower Danube.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fort-neoheroka"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Fort Neoheroka</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1713</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Fort Neoheroka was the decisive action of the Tuscarora War in North Carolina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-fredriksten.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Fredriksten</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1718</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Fredriksten was the siege of a Norwegian fortress by Swedish forces in 1718, during King Charles XII's invasion of Norway.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-isfahan"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Isfahan</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1722</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Isfahan was the siege of the Safavid capital by Afghan forces under Mahmud Hotaki in 1722.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-1099"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Jerusalem (1099)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1099</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Jerusalem in 1099 was the capture of Jerusalem by the armies of the First Crusade, followed by a massacre of many of its inhabitants.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-1187"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Jerusalem (1187)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1187</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Jerusalem in 1187 was the recapture of Jerusalem by Saladin after his victory over the Crusaders at Hattin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-587-bce"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Jerusalem (587 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 587 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Jerusalem in 587 BCE was the capture and destruction of Jerusalem by the Chaldean (Neo-Babylonian) king Nebuchadnezzar II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-597-bce"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Jerusalem (597 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 597 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Jerusalem in 597 BCE was the capture of Jerusalem by Nebuchadnezzar II of the Chaldean Empire after the kingdom of Judah rebelled.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-kawagoe-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-kawagoe-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Kawagoe Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Battle · Siege · 1545–1546</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Kawagoe Castle was a battle of Japan's Sengoku period in which the Later Hojo clan defended Kawagoe Castle in Musashi province against a large coalition of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-kerman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-kerman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Kerman</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1794</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Kerman was the final siege in 1794 in which the Qajar leader Agha Mohammad Khan captured the city of Kerman from Lotf Ali Khan, the last Zand ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-multan-1818"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Multan (1818)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1818</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Multan in 1818 was the capture of the city of Multan from its Afghan governor by the forces of Ranjit Singh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-odawara-1590"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-odawara-1590.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Odawara (1590)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1590</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Odawara of 1590 was Toyotomi Hideyoshi's campaign against the Later Hojo clan, whose refusal to submit was the last obstacle to his unification of Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Orléans</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1428–1429</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Orléans was the English siege of the city of Orléans on the Loire from October 1428 to May 1429, during the Hundred Years' War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-osaka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Osaka</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1614–1615</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Osaka was a series of campaigns in 1614–1615 in which the forces of Tokugawa Ieyasu attacked Osaka Castle, held by the Toyotomi family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-paris-885-886"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Paris (885–886)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 885–886</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Paris (885–886) was a siege of Paris by a large Viking army.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-pelium"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-pelium.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Pelium</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 335 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Pelium was fought in 335 BCE when Alexander the Great attacked the Illyrian king Cleitus at the fortress of Pelion on the western border of Macedon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-turin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-turin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Turin</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1706</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Turin was the French attempt in 1706 to capture the capital of Victor Amadeus II of Savoy during the War of the Spanish Succession.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-tyre-585-572-bce"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Tyre (585–572 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · c. 585 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Tyre was a long siege of the Phoenician island city of Tyre by Nebuchadnezzar II of the Chaldean Empire, begun c. 585 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-urshu"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Urshu</span><span class="enc-type">Siege</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Urshu was a siege by the Hittites of the city of Urshu in northern Syria, recorded in a Hittite text.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-uxellodunum"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Uxellodunum</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 51 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The siege of Uxellodunum in 51 BCE was the last major operation of Julius Caesar's conquest of Gaul.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-vicksburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-vicksburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Vicksburg</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1863</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Vicksburg, from May to 4 July 1863, ended with the surrender of the Confederate fortress on the Mississippi River in Mississippi to Union forces under…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-vienna-1529"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Vienna (1529)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1529</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Vienna in 1529 was the first Ottoman attempt to capture the Habsburg capital, led by Suleiman the Magnificent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-xiangyang"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Xiangyang</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1267–1273</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Xiangyang was a long siege of the twin cities of Xiangyang and Fancheng on the Han River from 1267 to 1273.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-yanmen"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Yanmen</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 615</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Yanmen was the encirclement of Emperor Yang of Sui by the Eastern Turkic ruler Shibi Khagan in 615, at Yanmen commandery in northern Shanxi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-yorktown"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-yorktown.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Yorktown</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1781</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Yorktown, in Virginia, was the last major battle of the American Revolutionary War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-tower"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-tower.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege tower</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A siege tower is a mobile wooden tower used to bring attackers to the top of fortification walls.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-warfare.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege warfare</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Siege warfare is the conduct of military operations to capture or defend fortified places, through blockade, assault, mining or bombardment.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siegen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siegen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siegen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Siegen is a university city in North Rhine-Westphalia, Germany, on the Sieg in the Siegerland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siegerland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siegerland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siegerland</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siegerland is a hilly region around the upper Sieg river in the south of North Rhine-Westphalia, centred on Siegen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siegfried-of-westerburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siegfried-of-westerburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siegfried of Westerburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Siegfried of Westerburg was archbishop of Cologne from 1275 until his death in 1297.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siegfried-count-of-luxembourg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siegfried-count-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siegfried, Count of Luxembourg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Siegfried (c. 922–998) was a Lotharingian count regarded as the founder of Luxembourg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siem-reap"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siem-reap.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siem Reap</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Siem Reap is a city in north-western Cambodia, capital of Siem Reap Province, near the ruins of Angkor.</span></a></li></ul>
